<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT A CELL IS AND WHAT IT SHOWS. The cell itself, the four ways a word can be
        written in one, the two marks that sit beside a word, and the templates the
        meaningful columns are drawn with. Not one value of its own - every number, colour
        and thickness below is a name declared in Values.xaml.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other three files: a value invented here would be as
        invisible as one invented in a view, and the guard treats all four alike.

        SPLIT OUT OF List.xaml ON 2026-08-12, AND ONE THING WAS REWRITTEN IN THE SAME STEP -
        said plainly, because the previous split's comment says "nothing rewritten" and
        copying that sentence here would have made it a lie. Everything below moved unchanged
        EXCEPT the two mark styles, which gained a shared base and lost an inheritance.

        That repair is why the split happened at all. List.xaml stood exactly on the 507-line
        markup ceiling, and `StartMark` could not be fixed without adding lines to it: it was
        BasedOn `StatusMark`, so it inherited triggers reading the RUN STATE, and an entry
        that was running wore a green fill in the column about its NEXT start. Backlog 166.
        A ceiling that forces a seam is the ceiling working.

        WHY THE SEAM IS HERE. List.xaml keeps what the list is BUILT FROM - the grid, the
        row and the template it owns, the headings. This keeps what a person actually READS -
        a cell, its words and its marks. Everything about a cell is on this side, which is
        what makes the name answerable without opening the file.

        NONE OF THE FOUR LOADS ON ITS OWN. This one reaches WPF UI's keys -
        DefaultDataGridCellStyle, BodyTextBlockStyle - and ours in Values.xaml, and
        StaticResource resolves both WHILE THE FILE IS BEING READ. So the merge order in
        App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then Values.xaml, then Controls.xaml, then
        List.xaml, then this. Anything that loads these files elsewhere has to do the same,
        which is why WpfHost and tools/wpfui-probe both build the stack in that order.

        LAST OF OURS, AND THAT IS A DIRECTION RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT. An implicit style
        merged later wins, so the most specialised half sits on top. Nothing here is implicit
        today, so the order costs nothing now and is the one that will still be right the day
        something is.
    -->

    <!--
        The cell carries spacing and, since 2026-08-24, its own template. It paints nothing at
        all: what fills a row is the row - see the block about it in List.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListCell" TargetType="DataGridCell" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridCellStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadListCell}" />
        <!--
            Against their MinHeight of 32. Row height is decided by the padding above, which is
            the same decision as the margins on the row: keep the density this list had.
        -->
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource SizeNone}" />

        <!--
            THE CELL OWNS ITS TEMPLATE SINCE 2026-08-24, THE LAST PLACE THIS LIST BORROWED ONE.
            Theirs wraps the ContentPresenter in a Border that paints a fully transparent brush and
            binds nothing else - one element per cell of every realised row, to be invisible.

            BasedOn IS KEPT, unlike what the row did in 2026-08-05: the row left because their
            TRIGGERS fought ours, and this one has none - read with tools/wpfui-probe, not assumed.
            It still carries KeyboardNavigation.IsTabStop=False, which the row's focus ring needs.
            Stretch is load-bearing too, because a TextBlock sized to its own text never reaches the
            width where CharacterEllipsis would trim it. AND IT TAKES NO MOUSE HITS - their border
            was what the pointer landed on in the padding, 106 of 603 sampled points, so List.xaml
            now supplies one transparent surface per row instead of one per cell. Backlog 221.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridCell">
                    <ContentPresenter Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                      HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                      VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                                      SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT EVERY CELL IN THE LIST IS MADE OF, AND THE TRIMMING IS THE POINT.

        Without it a cell too narrow for its text cuts it wherever the pixels run out, halfway
        through a character, with nothing to say it did - which reads as a rendering fault
        rather than as "this does not fit". Measured on the real window: a display name wanted
        476 pixels in a column of 365, and at 1039 pixels of window there is no width at which
        the longest of them fits. Something has to give and the person has to be able to see
        that it did.

        The tooltip is the other half of the same rule. Text that gives way has to be gettable -
        and ONLY such text, since 2026-08-25. Worked out when asked rather than bound on every
        cell of every row, which is what the switch below turns on. `CellTips` has the argument.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value=" " />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Numbers line up on their last digit, text on its first. Complaint 3 of the eleven, and
        it is how a column of process ids becomes comparable at a glance instead of being read
        one row at a time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellNumber" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        MONOSPACED, AND ONLY WHERE ALIGNMENT CARRIES MEANING - a launch path and a security
        descriptor. `docs/03` part 4 names those two and a hash: reading a hash in a proportional
        face is how two different hashes come to look the same, and the same is true of two paths
        differing by one character deep inside them.

        Everything else about it is CellText, including the trimming, which matters more here
        than anywhere: a descriptor is several hundred characters and will never fit.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellFixed" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE GEOMETRY EVERY MARK SHARES, AND IT CARRIES NO TRIGGER ON PURPOSE - 2026-08-12,
        backlog 166.

        Until today `StartMark` was BasedOn `StatusMark`, which meant it inherited every one of
        that style's triggers - and those read `StatusShape`, the RUN STATE. A running entry hit
        the "running" trigger, which sets Fill and Stroke; the start column's own "disabled"
        trigger then set Stroke and left the Fill alone. So an entry that was running and
        disabled wore a GREEN FILLED DOT in the column about its next start. Measured on the
        pixel before the repair: #6CCB5F, the same green as the status dot two columns left.

        A mark must answer about its own column and nothing else. The way to guarantee that is
        for the shared half to be geometry with no opinion about any field, which is this style,
        and for each mark to carry its own triggers and only its own. `MarkDistinctionGuards`
        holds the rule now - it walks the BasedOn chain, which is exactly what it could not see
        before, because it read a style's own triggers and never its inherited ones.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="MarkShape" TargetType="Ellipse">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAfterShape}" />
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource ShapeOutline}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE MARK BESIDE A MEANING, AND FILLED VERSUS HOLLOW IS NOT DECORATION.

        Filled means the entry is doing something - running, or on its way somewhere. Hollow
        means it is not. Colour separates within each pair, so the shape carries the coarse
        answer and the colour the fine one. WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.1 says colour may not be the only
        visual means of conveying information, and the word beside the mark is the third
        channel and always present.

        The trigger compares against a CODE from the view model, never against the word. The
        word is translated; the code is not, and a trigger matching on "Running" would quietly
        stop matching the day a second language file appears - the row would just lose its
        colour, with nothing anywhere saying why. This project has had four of those.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StatusMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource MarkShape}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.running">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRunning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.transit">
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.stopped">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.paused">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                Explicit rather than left to the style's default, which drew it identically to a
                stopped entry. Backlog 149 - the mark now differs in shape, not in a shade of grey
                nobody can separate.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StatusShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The start type's mark, and it is Hidden rather than Collapsed for the ordinary case.
        Collapsed would give the space back and every word in the column would sit at a
        different place depending on whether its entry had something to report, which is a
        ragged column pretending to be information.

        Waiting on a trigger deliberately gets NO mark. It is not a fault - it is why four of
        the ten entries that read as "automatic and not running" on a real machine were fine.
        It is said in words, next to the type, where it explains rather than accuses.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="StartMark" TargetType="Ellipse" BasedOn="{StaticResource MarkShape}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Hidden" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <!--
                Each mark says what it means, backlog 149. Colour was the only carrier until
                2026-08-10 and docs/11 forbids exactly that - the owner reported seeing dots that
                were sometimes empty and sometimes red with no way to find out why.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.missing">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.missing}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.disabled">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.disabled}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.unknown}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                THE FOUR START TYPES, 2026-08-17, owner's decision - until then all four were drawn
                as nothing at all, so the mark in this column answered "is something wrong" and
                never "what is this set to".

                RINGS, NOT FILLS, AND THE THREE TRIGGERS ABOVE ARE WHY. A filled mark here means
                the manager cannot carry out the setting - only the missing file earns that, and it
                is declared first so it wins. These four set Stroke and leave Fill alone, which the
                base style has already put at Transparent.

                Their colours are argued in Themes/Colours.xaml, where they are measured: quiet on
                purpose, separated from each other by calculation, and none of them anywhere near
                the green a running service wears two columns to the left. That last one is backlog
                166, which was exactly this picture arriving by accident.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.boot">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartBoot}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.boot}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.system">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartSystem}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.system}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.automatic">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartAutomatic}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.automatic}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StartShape}" Value="shape.start.manual">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource StartManual}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.start.mark.manual}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE THIRD MARK, AND IT IS A TRIANGLE RATHER THAN A DOT ON PURPOSE - backlog 165.

        The column answering "is this entry doing the opposite of its start type" carried the word
        Yes or No alone, between two columns that both wear marks, and `docs/11` 3.1 calls a word
        without a shape insufficient wherever something means something.

        WHY NOT A THIRD DOT. A dot in that position reads as one more answer about the running
        state - which is the confusion the start column was repaired for on the same day. This is a
        PERSISTENT disagreement between two settings rather than a state something is passing
        through, so it gets its own shape family and it does not borrow the amber that means "on
        its way somewhere" either.

        WHY THE COLOUR IS BORROWED AND NOT INVENTED. MeaningUntrusted already carries "something
        here is wrong" for a missing file, and every brush in this product has a declared contrast
        threshold that `ContrastGuards` enforces - a new colour would be a new threshold to argue
        rather than a shape to draw. The two marks that share it are never in the same column and
        differ in shape, which is the rule `MarkDistinctionGuards` holds.

        Only a definite YES is marked. An entry doing what its start type says is the ordinary case,
        and marking seven hundred ordinary rows is how a mark stops meaning anything.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="AgainstMark" TargetType="Path">
        <Setter Property="Data" Value="{StaticResource ShapeAgainst}" />
        <Setter Property="Stretch" Value="Uniform" />
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ShapeMark}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginAfterShape}" />
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource ShapeOutline}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Hidden" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
        <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding AgainstShape}" Value="shape.against">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUntrusted}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.against.mark.yes}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                A broken outline for broken knowledge, the same rule the two dots keep - and the
                only shape in any of the three columns that is not a whole one.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding AgainstShape}" Value="shape.unknown">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{StaticResource MeaningUnknown}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeDashArray" Value="{StaticResource ShapeUnreadableOutline}" />
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{DynamicResource gui.against.mark.unknown}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE TWO CELLS THAT CARRY A MARK AS WELL AS A WORD, AND THEY MOVED HERE FROM THE MIDDLE OF
        MainWindow.xaml ON 2026-08-11. `docs/11` 3.1 asks for shape AND colour AND label and calls
        any two of the three insufficient, which is why these two columns are templated at all.

        <b>They are here rather than in the view because the columns are built in code now.</b>
        A template written inline inside a column definition has no name, so nothing can ask for
        it - and S6d2 turns seventeen columns into a list somebody chooses from, which means the
        columns are constructed rather than declared. A resource dictionary is the one place a
        column built in code can reach.

        THE MARK AND THE WORD SIT IN A GRID, NOT A StackPanel, and that is load-bearing: a
        horizontal StackPanel measures its children against infinite width, so the text would
        never learn it has to give way and the ellipsis would never appear. The trimming would be
        present, correct and dead - which is the shape of fault this window has shipped four times.

        THE WORD COMES THROUGH THE INDEXER AND THE MARK THROUGH A PROPERTY, and that is not an
        inconsistency. The word is a cell, so it is asked for by the column's identifier like
        every other cell in the list. The mark is not a cell - it sits beside one - and it is a
        code rather than a word precisely so that a trigger never compares against translated text.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="StatusCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource StatusMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [status]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <DataTemplate x:Key="StartTypeCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource StartMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [startType]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        The third one, same shape of cell as the two above - mark, then word, in a Grid rather than
        a StackPanel for the reason spelled out over them.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="AgainstStartTypeCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Path Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource AgainstMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [runsAgainstItsStartType]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>
</ResourceDictionary>
